<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Spec 31 §3/§5: the macOS runtime signing profile (fixed set, pinned
  here until spec 31 §2's manifest derivation lands).

  allow-dyld-environment-variables — THE LOAD-BEARING GRANT
    (tebako#1117; the tebako-runtime-r#5 precedent). The spec 22
    tier-1 visibility design re-execs the exe with the driver-armed
    DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES (build/resources/tebako_php_main.c's
    second incarnation — the in-image libtfs_preload.dylib is the
    UNPATCHED php interpreter's only VFS visibility). The hardened
    runtime strips every DYLD_* variable at launch unless this grant
    is present; the re-exec'd incarnation then runs shimless, the
    mount root (/__tfs__) does not exist on the host filesystem, and
    every in-image read fails — an entry script dies "Could not open
    input file" while `php -v` keeps answering (the version fast
    path never touches the image) — the exact tebako-runtime-r#5
    symptom class, one interpreter over. Proven on darwin-23
    (macOS 14.1.1 arm64) against the PUBLISHED v0.1.0 pair itself:
    its exe carries this hardened profile without the grant, the
    re-exec'd incarnation shows no DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES, the
    mounted env image is invisible to the interpreter
    (is_dir("/__tfs__") false), and a payload entry inside the
    mount dies "Could not open input file"; the same published exe
    re-signed adding ONLY this grant runs the same probe green.
    The macos-15 runners TOLERATE a missing grant (their dyld keeps
    the variables — how v0.1.0 shipped green), so no CI runner can
    police this class at exec time: ci/macos-sign-notarize-runtime.sh
    asserts the embedded profile statically, by key name.
  disable-library-validation — EVERY runtime slice: a hardened-runtime
    process by default refuses unsigned dylibs, and the driver
    materializes in-image native objects (the preload shim, .so
    extensions) to the host for dlopen; they carry no Developer ID
    signature of their own (§1.4). This grant governs only WHICH
    dylibs may load — it says nothing about WHETHER dyld honors the
    environment; only the grant above does (the two-key profile's
    comment conflated the two, which is how v0.1.0 shipped signed
    WITHOUT the load-bearing grant).
  allow-jit — a grant, never a requirement: this factory builds no JIT
    (php's opcache — the only php JIT surface — is off under the
    disable-all configure line), so the entitlement is inert today;
    it rides the profile so an opcache-enabled line never needs a
    profile re-review.

  Keep each <true/> on its own line: the embedding check in
  ci/macos-sign-notarize-runtime.sh counts them AND greps each key
  name (macOS 15 emits the embedded plist single-line).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
